# 0.6.4 - ERR-ADOPT-1, THE FAILURE COLUMN REACHES ZERO. The nine rows
# below waited on one question - may `println`'s effect row widen? - and
# the answer is a measurement recorded in docs/error-model.md section
# 10.1: with `sysWriteFd` ported alone the row widens from `IO` to
# `Alloc,IO` on eleven functions in the compiler's whole closure, six
# public, and ZERO `restrict(no-alloc)` claims are refused, because
# what widens it is the FAILURE path building an `Error` while the
# success path is two registers read by a direct match (the box moved
# to the caller on 2026-09-03, docs/error-model.md §10).
# So `println` may allocate, and only when a write fails, and its row
# says so. `sysWriteAllFd` and `writeStr` keep the Int channel that
# `println`'s 804 expansions are; that channel is a separate decision.
#
# The nine read CHANGED where the row already carried Alloc and WIDENED
# where it was IO alone; the rows after them are the callers the widening
