# 0.7.6 - SUBTYPE-ADOPT-1: `strFromByte` takes a `Byte`.
# `strFromByte` stored only the low eight bits, so a caller with 256
# or more got a silently truncated byte. Its parameter is
# `(subtype Byte is Int range 0 .. 256)` now, and an out-of-range
# argument traps with status 80 at the call boundary instead. Every
# in-tree caller passes a value already in range (two constants, a
# percent-decoded byte, two hex-digit spellings, two base-36 digits),
# so none of them moves. A caller that relied on the truncation masks
# first and casts: `(strFromByte (cast Byte (& b 255)))`.
0.7.6  F  strFromByte  (-> Int String) -> (-> Byte String): truncation is now a trap, not a silent wrap

# 0.7.7 - MM-EXEC-9c: `__retain` and `__release` joined `Unsafe`
# (6527bea0). Five public wrappers whose only raw operation is a
# release answered an EMPTY row at 0.7.6 and answer `#effects=Unsafe`
# now. The widening reaches callers, because `Unsafe` flows through
# rows like any effect (only AX3073, the "say `effect(unsafe)`" rule,
# stops at the wrapper): a caller tagged `pure` or
# `restrict(no-unsafe)` that frees a container compiled at 0.7.6 and
# is refused now, AX3010 / AX3049. That is the point - handing a
# share back is what makes a later use-after-free possible - and the
# remedy is to drop the claim from the freeing function, or to free
# outside it.
